// Test: bugfixes/ssr_concat_in_call_arg_no_oom
// Regression: CMP-SSR-MALLOC-OOM-PAGE-RENDER (Step 3.5 — call-arg routing)
//
// The Step 3 fix (commit 42c63311) routed body-local string-concat
// assignments through the transient arena. It missed concats that
// appear as *function-call arguments* — e.g.
//
//   string idx = i.toString()
//   string preamble = json.get(examplesJson, idx + ".preamble")
//   string code     = json.get(examplesJson, idx + ".code")
//   ... etc
//
// In Web Site Clean `app/ui/web/pages/tutorials/[id].cln::buildExamples`
// this shape produced 4 unrouted `string.concat` calls per iteration
// (one per JSON-path lookup) that still allocated on the main heap.
// Across ~50 iters × ~30 tutorials the residual leak still tripped the
// 128 MB bump cap — same byte-count signature (~16264 B) as the
// pre-Step-3 failure.
//
// Step 3.5 (this test's gate) extends `rewrite_body_local_helpers_to_transient`
// with a recursive walker that finds every `BinaryOp::Add`/`StringConcat`
// chain inside a `Call`/`MethodCall`/`NamespaceCall`/`StaticMethodCall`/
// `Constructor` argument list and rewrites it to `string_concat_transient`.
// Safety: a value produced as a function-call argument is consumed by
// the call; its lifetime ends at the call's return, strictly before
// the surrounding iteration boundary. Routing the arg's allocation
// through `__transient_alloc` cannot dangle anything.
//
// This test exercises two shapes the call-arg walker MUST handle:
//
//   1. Concat as MethodCall argument:
//        idx.contains("." + suffix)
//      — exercises the MethodCall branch and the receiver descent.
//
//   2. Nested: concat inside a parenthesized concat that is itself a
//      method receiver:
//        ("[" + idx + "]").length()
//      — exercises the recursive descent through BinaryOp::Add into
//      MethodCall's receiver.
//
// Both call sites' arg-concats must route through
// `string_concat_transient`. Without Step 3.5 these allocate on the
// main heap each iteration; with Step 3.5 they land in the transient
// pool and are reclaimed at end-of-iter by `__transient_scope_exit`.
//
// Verification: wasm-tools print on the compiled WASM shows ZERO calls
// to `string.concat` (id 14) coming from this loop, and N calls to
// `string_concat_transient` instead. (The non-loop top-level call sites
// in the surrounding test driver are not subject to the rewrite.)
//
// Output: 5000 (acc accumulates one "a" per iteration).

start:
	string acc = ""
	integer i = 0
	while i < 5000
		string idx = i.toString()
		string suffix = "x"
		// Shape 1: concat as MethodCall argument.
		boolean has_dot = idx.contains("." + suffix)
		// Shape 2: concat as MethodCall *receiver* (nested in another concat).
		integer len = ("[" + idx + "]").length()
		// Accumulator self-append at the top of the body (matcher
		// requirement). The conditional usage of `has_dot`/`len` is
		// just sink-code so DCE can't eliminate the concat args.
		acc = acc + "a"
		if has_dot
			i = i
		if len > 0
			i = i
		i = i + 1
	printl(acc.length().toString())
